    "done_when": "Read section 31 of crew-observability-truth and its comment c-cot-s31-exit-half first; the exit-record half landed at 68be6145c. First establish whether the resume half already holds at main. tests/test_direct_resume_needs_an_observed_death.py and tests/test_resume_sweep_needs_an_observed_death.py exist; record in the evidence what they cover and what they miss. Then make sure a resume of a run whose worker process is alive is refused before anything launches, with a refusal naming that pid, and that the run keeps classifying as working through the refusal. This covers both the manual reckon crew resume (reckon/crew/resumption.py) and the automatic resume sweep (reckon/crew/recovery.py). Fold in the exit-half review's first follow-on: when the launcher is killed by signal but the worker it adopted lives on, no exit record is written early. tests/test_resume_refuses_a_live_worker.py, in a temporary RECKON_HOME, uses a real short-lived child process as the live worker, bounded by its own timeout and ended by its pid. It asserts both resume paths refuse, naming the pid, and that classification stays working. The two existing resume tests and tests/test_attempt_exit_waits_for_its_worker.py keep passing. Record the outcome in docs/evidence/fragments/crew-observability-truth/resume-refuses-a-live-worker.html. Run `date -u` first and treat that instant as your start; your deadline is 60 minutes later. Write your manifest before starting any long command. Run both gate arms with pytest-xdist (-n 8 --timeout=300), never serially. Run the base arm, from a `git archive` extraction of your base under /tmp in a directory named for this run, and then the head arm, each in the foreground: a background command dies when your turn ends. Keep the gate to the test files that reach your change, tens of files rather than hundreds. Each arm uses its own basetemp outside any repository and writes to its own new log path, which it never overwrites. Each arm is recorded as